      "heading": "Three ideas, and what they do not cover",
      "narration": "You have now done, by hand, everything this library does. You built a neuron, you derived the chain rule through two layers, you wrote the four-step loop, and you tuned an optimizer. So it is worth being precise about what a framework actually buys you, because the honest answer is narrower than the marketing and much more useful to know. In the backprop lab you derived gradients for a two-layer scalar network. That was six lines of careful algebra and it was tractable. Now picture a network of forty layers with attention and normalization and residual connections, and picture re-deriving all of it every time you move a layer or change an activation. It is not hard mathematics — it is the same chain rule — it is just thousands of terms, and every one is a chance to make a sign error you will not find for a week. That specific labour is what a framework removes. It removes the bookkeeping. It does not remove the thinking. PyTorch is three ideas, and once you see them the rest of the library is vocabulary. The first is the tensor: an n-dimensional array, very much like numpy, with two extra abilities we will come to. The second is autograd: while your forward pass runs, PyTorch quietly records every operation it performs, and that recording is enough to replay the whole thing backwards and hand you every gradient. The third is nn dot Module: a container that holds layers and, crucially, knows which of its own numbers are parameters, so you never have to keep a list of them yourself. Tensors, autograd, Modules. Everything in the last five parts of this course is those three things arranged differently. Now the part people skip. The framework does not automate the mathematics, the training loop, or the debugging. When you call loss dot backward, PyTorch computes the correct gradients of whatever you actually wrote — and it will do that perfectly for a loss function that is wrong, for data you forgot to shuffle, for a learning rate ten times too high, and for a model that is quietly training on its own labels. It has no opinion about any of that. Everything you learned in the last three lessons is what tells you the numbers coming out are sensible, and that judgement is the part that stays yours. One last idea, and it explains why this library rather than the others. PyTorch is define-by-run: the computation graph is built by executing your Python code, line by line, as it runs. There is no separate compile step and no session to submit work to. Which means an if statement inside your forward pass is legal, a Python for loop over a sequence is legal, and both are differentiable, because the graph simply records what actually happened this time. And it means you debug a model the way you debug any other Python: put a print in the middle and look. That single design decision is why the research world moved here, and it is why the code in the rest of this course is readable.",
