      "heading": "Autograd: the tape, and the four calls that matter",
      "narration": "This is the idea that made the library, and having derived backprop by hand you are in the rare position of being able to see exactly what it is doing. When you create a tensor with requires-grad set to true, PyTorch starts watching it. Every operation you then perform on it — a multiply, an addition, a ReLU, a matrix product — is recorded onto what is usually called a tape: a record of which operation happened and what its inputs were. The forward pass builds this tape as a side effect of simply running. Then you call backward on your loss, and PyTorch walks that tape from the end to the beginning, applying at each step exactly the local derivative rule you worked out in the Baratie lesson, multiplying as it goes, and depositing the final gradient for each parameter into that parameter's dot-grad attribute. That is the whole mechanism. It is your lab, automated, and it is not doing anything you have not already done on paper. Now the behaviour that catches everybody, and it catches them silently. Gradients accumulate. When backward runs, it adds to dot-grad rather than overwriting it. That is a deliberate design decision and it is genuinely useful: it is how you train on a batch too large to fit in memory, by running several small forward and backward passes and letting their gradients pile up before you step. But it means that if you forget to clear them, step number ten is being taken using the sum of the gradients from all ten steps, your effective learning rate grows without limit, and your loss goes to infinity in a way that looks like an exploding gradient and is not. This is why optimizer dot zero-grad sits at the top of every training loop you will ever read. It is not boilerplate. It is load-bearing. Next, the two ways to switch autograd off, because you do not always want the tape. Wrap a block in with torch dot no-grad, and nothing inside it is recorded. Use that for validation and for inference, where you have no intention of calling backward and the tape is pure wasted memory — on a large model it is most of your memory. And use dot detach when you want to cut one particular tensor loose from the graph while everything else keeps recording; the classic case is taking a value out to log it, or stopping a gradient from flowing back into part of a model you have decided to freeze. Finally, the error message you will meet within your first week. The tape is freed as soon as backward has used it, because keeping it would leak memory on every iteration. So if you call backward twice on the same graph, you get: tried to backward through the graph a second time. Ninety percent of the time this does not mean what it says. It means a tensor from the previous iteration is still being held in a Python variable — you appended the loss itself to a list instead of loss dot item, say — and so the old graph is still alive and got dragged into this iteration's backward pass. Look for the tensor you forgot to let go of.",
