      "heading": "Franky's dock: the jigs hold the tolerance",
      "narration": "Here is the picture for the whole lesson. Think about how Usopp makes a part. He cuts it by eye and by feel, checks it against the gap, shaves a little more off, checks again. It works. He learns the wood while he does it, and he knows exactly why the finished part is the shape it is, because he made every decision on the way. And it takes him a morning. Now walk into Franky's dock. There is a jig for that cut. You clamp the stock in, you run the lathe, and the part comes out to the same tolerance every time, in minutes rather than hours, all day, without the drift that creeps into a tired hand. Nobody in that dock would go back to cutting by eye. But notice what the jig is and is not. The jig does not know what ship is being built. It does not know whether this part belongs to a keel or a railing. It holds an angle, and that is the entirety of its contribution. Which means if you set it to the wrong angle, with complete confidence, it will reproduce your mistake perfectly — a hundred identical wrong parts, produced faster than Usopp could have made one right one, all of them looking beautifully finished. The tool has removed the possibility of a random error and has done nothing whatsoever about a systematic one. So the shipwright's job does not disappear. It moves. It moves from cutting accurately to deciding what to cut, and to the thing Franky actually does all day, which is walking the dock and noticing that a run of parts is coming out subtly wrong before four hundred of them exist. That is exactly the trade you are making with PyTorch, and I want you to hold it clearly, because it is the difference between the people who get good at this and the people who stay stuck. Autograd holds the tolerance on the arithmetic. It will not make a sign error, ever, and that alone is worth the whole library. But it has no opinion about your loss function, your data, your evaluation split, or whether the number going down means the thing you care about is going up. And the speed cuts both ways: a wrong decision now costs you a thousand wrong parts instead of one, and it costs them in twenty minutes on a machine you are paying for by the hour. Everything you learned by hand in the last three lessons is what lets you walk the dock. The library is the jig. You are still the shipwright.",
